# A function's own halving loop written as iterate applied to the function
# (the inlined hot shape), so that wren_typed_specialization's
# static argument lemma, which matches iterate with a function argument,
# gives the loop its typed branch, binder filters included. The own loop of
# G is the recursion
#
#   fixpoint (λself. λn. λs. n s (λm. self m (self m s)) (λm. G (self m (self m s))))
#
# (direct spelling; the respelled one runs G first in the odd case), and
# iterate is the same recursion with the function as a parameter:
#
#   fixpoint (λself. λn. λg. λs. n s (λm. self m g (self m g s)) (λm. g (self m g (self m g s)))).
#
# Each lemma is an instance of static_argument_fixpoint (static_arguments), read from
# right to left and applied to the state Y: with
# K = λt. λn. λg. λs. n s (λm. t m (t m s)) (λm. g (t m (t m s))) (direct),
# that lemma's left side, fixpoint (λS. λN. λG. K (λM. S M G) N G) X H, is
# iterate X H up to β, and its right side, fixpoint (λT. λN. K T N H) X, is
# the own loop of H applied to X up to β. Both sides here are written in β
# normal form, so each lemma is that lemma's equation under the congruence
# of application and β; it is a named axiom citing the same paper proof.
# Written on wren_fixpoint_by_value, the form the typed member's lets reduce
# fixpoint to (wren_typed_specialization), so it runs after a round of that
# module.
#
# Termination of this module's saturation, derived and not proved: each
# lemma's left side is a recursion of two parameters whose body has the own
# loop's shape, and its right side a recursion of three parameters, which
# matches neither left side. So the instances are at most the own loops in
# the input graph.
#
